<GuidePage
  canonicalPath="/guides/how-much-extra-backing/"
  index={false}
  title="Quilt Backing Overage: How Much Extra Backing to Add"
  description="Calculate backing size by adding overage on every side, understand the 4-inch planning default, and confirm longarm requirements before buying."
>
  <section>
    <h2>Backing must be larger than the quilt top</h2>
    <p>
      Extra backing gives room for basting, shifting, and holding the quilt
      during quilting. QuiltClarity’s editable default is 4 inches on every
      side. That means adding 8 inches to both the quilt-top width and
      length—not 4 inches total.
    </p>
    <p>
      For example, a 60 × 72 inch quilt top with 4 inches per side needs a
      backing at least 68 × 80 inches before its panels are planned.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section>
    <h2>Longarm requirements take priority</h2>
    <p>
      Four inches per side is a conservative planning default, not a universal
      rule. Frame style, leaders, clamps, quilting density, and the longarmer’s
      workflow can change the required margin. If a professional will quilt the
      project, ask for their minimum backing dimensions before purchasing or
      piecing the backing.
    </p>
    <p>
      Measure the completed top rather than relying only on pattern dimensions.
      Squaring, borders, and piecing variation can change the real size.
    </p>
    <p>
      Published conventions also vary; compare{' '}
      <a href="https://quiltkeeperstudio.com/calculators/backing"
        >QuiltKeeper's backing guidance</a
      >, then follow your own quilter's requirement.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section>
    <h2>Overage is separate from panel yardage</h2>
    <p>
      After overage establishes the required backing rectangle, fabric panels
      still have to cover it. Joined edges lose width to seam allowance, and a
      horizontal-seam layout can consume a different length than a vertical-seam
      layout. Directional prints may rule out one orientation.
    </p>
    <p>
      The{' '}<a href="/calculators/quilt-backing/">quilt backing calculator</a
      >{' '}compares those valid panel directions. Review{' '}<a
        href="/guides/width-of-fabric/">usable width of fabric</a
      >{' '}before entering the bolt width, or use the{' '}<a
        href="/calculators/quilt-binding/">binding calculator</a
      >{' '}once the quilt dimensions are settled.
    </p>
  </section>
</GuidePage>
